{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git log --oneline -4 -- docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md; git rev-parse --short HEAD; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md'","aggregated_output":"e101932 docs(space): gate the index note's ref-type assertion to the JVM\n0bd7550 docs(space): pin resumed index sessions strong, not just fresh ones\n280dda2 docs(space): fold the glm runtime review into the index observer note\n5296ee5 docs: state the peer-observer invariant and close the index note's gaps\ne101932\n     1\t# dao.space.index as a dao.stream Observer\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design note, 2026-09-13. `dao.space.index` is already the dao.stream\n     4\tobserver on the dao.space side: `snapshot-datoms` + `publish-index!` is one\n     5\tobserver run — attach at `:oldest`, fold to `blocked`, publish, keep nothing.\n     6\tThis note makes it a *stateful* observer, driven by\n     7\t`dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` over *any* `dao.stream` medium. No\n     8\tnew namespace: everything here is index realization, which `dao.space.index`\n     9\talready owns. The library specified here is payload-agnostic: it sees d5 rows\n    10\tand a supplied ref schema, and nothing below knows or cares what the rows\n    11\tmean or who else reads the medium. Subordinate to\n    12\t[`dao.space.md`](./dao.space.md) (write path, three boundaries),\n    13\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) (§Composition), and the observer\n    14\tcoordination already specified by `dao.stream.observer`. It\n    15\tcomposes with [`yin.vm.macro.md`](./yin.vm.macro.md) §4.2, whose \"commit\n    16\t`program-in` first for durable provenance\" becomes an instance of this\n    17\tnote.\n    18\t\n    19\t**Why this note exists** — a composition it enables, not a fact the index\n    20\tknows: attach a `yin.vm` evaluator and `dao.space.index` to the same medium,\n    21\tand the VM constructs CESK state while the index materializes covered\n    22\tindexes over the same batches, so `dao.space.query/q` answers Datalog over\n    23\tthe program being executed, as it grows. Neither observer is aware of the\n    24\tother; the stream is the only coupling, and the index's ignorance of what it\n    25\tindexes is as load-bearing as the VM's ignorance of being indexed. That\n    26\tinvariant belongs to the composition and is stated where compositions are\n    27\t(`datom.world.md`, `dao.space.md` §Three Boundaries); this document\n    28\tspecifies only the index side.\n    29\t\n    30\t---\n    31\t\n    32\t## 0. Decisions\n    33\t\n    34\t1. **dao.space observes; it does not have to be the writer.** Today\n    35\t   indexing is \"the writer's duty\" over the writer's own local stream\n    36\t   (`dao.space.md` §The Write Path). That stays true *as a composition*, but\n    37\t   the mechanism becomes an observer over a medium — any medium, whoever\n    38\t   writes it and whoever else reads it.\n    39\t2. **One coordination loop, one library.** `dao.space.index` is driven by\n    40\t   `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` with its state in the `:consumer`\n    41\t   slot, exactly as an evaluator or the macro expander is. No second\n    42\t   observer machinery, and no new namespace: \"the dao.space observer\" is a\n    43\t   role `dao.space.index` plays when so driven, not a module.\n    44\t3. **The index is incremental and persistent.** Each observed batch is\n    45\t   folded into the four covered indexes as `dao.data.btree` values; the\n    46\t   trees are structurally shared across batches. `publish-index!`'s\n    47\t   full-rebuild-from-origin becomes a consequence of *starting* an observer\n    48\t   at `:oldest`, not the only way to build an index.\n    49\t4. **The observer never rewrites a datom and never allocates `t`.**\n    50\t   Transaction time is the writer's (the transactor's). The observer records\n    51\t   what it saw and *where* (its own batch ordinal) as facts *about* the\n    52\t   batch, in its own metadata namespace, never by editing rows.\n    53\t5. **Identity resolution is the observer's reading.** Tempids are\n    54\t   batch-local; the observer assigns durable ids per batch and records the\n    55\t   mapping as **resolution facts**. This is the one place cross-medium\n    56\t   identity can exist, because dao.space is the one observer that may read\n    57\t   every medium.\n    58\t6. **The index is payload-agnostic.** It interprets d5 shape and the\n    59\t   supplied ref schema, nothing else: it never evaluates, never inspects an\n    60\t   attribute's meaning, and never learns what another observer of the same\n    61\t   medium does with the rows. (This is what rules out the\n    62\t   \"CESK-in-the-index\" premise of `yin.vm-in-dao.space.md`: two interpreters\n    63\t   over one stream, not one interpreter with two faces.)\n    64\t\n    65\t---\n    66\t\n    67\t## 1. Today, and the gap\n    68\t\n    69\tAll of `dao.space` is on `dao.stream` (no v1 references remain). Its\n    70\tstream use has three shapes:\n    71\t\n    72\t| Piece | Shape today |\n    73\t|---|---|\n    74\t| `dao.space.transactor` | write-side interpreter over an agent's *own* local memory-log; wraps each transaction in one `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record; derives `t` by scanning the whole retained history at `create!` |\n    75\t| `dao.space.index/publish-index!` | `snapshot-datoms` the local stream **in full from `:oldest`**, build four btrees from scratch, append node blobs then manifest to an intake stream |\n    76\t| `dao.space.query/snapshot` | the read side's one v2 interpreter: mint `:oldest`, loop `observe/step` into a relation, stop at `blocked`/`ended`/`gap`/`defect`; a **one-shot** |\n    77\t\n    78\tThree consequences follow, and they are the gap:\n    79\t\n    80\t- **Nothing indexes a medium dao.space did not write.** Any other writer's\n    81\t  medium — a program stream, an expander's log — carries datoms nobody\n    82\t  indexes; querying them means a full `snapshot` each time, which is\n    83\t  Datalog *as of* a call, never over a medium as it grows.\n    84\t- **No index is incremental.** Every publication rebuilds from the origin;\n    85\t  every `create!` replays history for the watermark. The transactor's own\n    86\t  *Open items* names this as O(history) and asks for \"one truth — the log\n    87\t  carries its own checkpoint\". An observer that holds a persistent index\n    88\t  *is* that checkpoint's natural owner.\n    89\t- **`snapshot` is not a session.** It mints a fresh cursor per call and\n    90\t  cannot be resumed batch by batch, so it cannot participate in a\n    91\t  `run-on-stream` round the way an evaluator does.\n    92\t\n    93\t**Already true.** `dao.space.index` is the consumer side of observation.\n    94\t`snapshot-datoms` + `publish-index!` *is* an observer run: attach at\n    95\t`:oldest`, fold everything to `blocked`, publish once, discard the state —\n    96\tthe degenerate case of `run-on-stream` with a consumer that is always ready\n    97\tand keeps nothing between calls. `datoms-from-elements` already accepts both\n    98\telement shapes (raw d5 rows and transaction records), `index-datoms` already\n    99\tbuilds the in-memory index, and `observe/step` already exists.\n   100\t\n   101\t**Not yet true**, and it is code, not wording:\n   102\t\n   103\t1. *Incremental fold.* `index-datoms` builds a whole index from a whole datom\n   104\t   vector; `publish-index!` rebuilds from the origin every time. Being\n   105\t   driven batch by batch needs `fold-batch : index-state batch →\n   106\t   index-state'` over persistent btree values (§2.2).\n   107\t2. *Tempid resolution across batches.* A transactor-written medium carries\n   108\t   resolved ids; a medium written batch by batch without a transactor\n   109\t   carries per-batch negative tempids that *repeat*. `dao.space.index` never\n   110\t   meets that case today because it\n   111\t   only indexes the writer's own resolved log. Observing a medium it did not\n   112\t   write requires the per-batch allocator and resolution facts (§3) — a\n   113\t   correctness requirement, not a framing one.\n   114\t3. *Bad input as data.* `snapshot-datoms` throws on the first malformed\n   115\t   element, which is right for a one-shot over your own log and\n   116\t   head-of-line-blocks a session over someone else's medium (§2.2 step 1).\n   117\t\n   118\t---\n   119\t\n   120\t## 2. The index consumer (`dao.space.index`)\n   121\t\n   122\t### 2.1 The index state\n   123\t\n   124\t```\n   125\tindex-state = {:indexes  {:eavt bt :aevt bt :avet bt :vaet bt}   ; dao.data.btree values, structurally shared across batches\n   126\t               :storage  <IStorage over a content handle>       ; the trees' storage; persists across publishes (§4.1)\n   127\t               :mode     :resolved | :unresolved                 ; fixed at construction (§3.1)\n   128\t               :ids      nil | {:next-eid n}                     ; allocator; present only in :unresolved mode\n   129\t               :batch    n                                       ; ordinal of the next batch, observer-local\n   130\t               :schema   {attr {:db/valueType :db.type/ref ...}} ; supplied; which attrs are refs\n   131\t               :publish  nil | {:intake writer :staged nil|payloads}   ; optional, §4\n   132\t               :defects  []}                                     ; malformed batches seen since last drain\n   133\t\n   134\t;; all in dao.space.index, beside index-datoms and publish-index!\n   135\tready?         (fn [x] (nil? (get-in x [:publish :staged])))\n   136\tload           index/fold-batch                                ; pure; §2.2\n   137\trun            index/flush-staged                              ; retries a staged publication; identity otherwise (§4)\n   138\tindex/publish! index-state → index-state'                      ; explicit: stage this state's trees, then flush (§4)\n   139\tindex/drain    (fn [x] [(assoc x :defects []) (:defects x)])\n   140\t```\n   141\t\n   142\tDriven as `(run-on-stream {:observer o :consumer index-state} ready?\n   143\tindex/fold-batch index/flush-staged)` over any `dao.stream` reader\n   144\thandle attached through `dao.stream.observer/attach`. The coordination\n   145\tinspects no field of the index state; it drives it as readily as a VM —\n   146\twhich is the point of decision 2. **Publication is an explicit composition\n   147\tstep**, not a policy inside the loop: the composition calls `publish!`\n   148\tbetween rounds (after every *n* rounds, on `blocked`, on a timer — its\n   149\tchoice), and only the *retry* of a staged publication that answered `full`\n   150\tlives inside `run`, so that `ready?` stays false until the intake accepted\n   151\tit. Calling `publish!` while a publication is still staged is a caller\n   152\terror (the composition checks `ready?` first); the staged payload list is\n   153\tnever replaced, so at-most-once per manifest holds.\n   154\t\n   155\t### 2.2 `fold-batch`\n   156\t\n   157\tOne batch, one pure fold:\n   158\t\n   159\t1. **Admit elements.** Each element is a d5 row or a\n   160\t   `{:dao.space/transaction {:t :datoms}}` record, flattened. Admission is\n   161\t   *looser than* `datom/local-datom?`, which `element-datoms` applies today\n   162\t   and which requires a non-negative `e`: in `:unresolved` mode admission is\n   163\t   exactly `local-datom?` minus that one clause — integer `e` of either\n   164\t   sign, namespaced keyword `a`, `t ≥ 0`, integer `m`. `v` is unconstrained\n   165\t   by `local-datom?` already, so a negative ref-valued `v` needs no schema\n   166\t   knowledge to be *admitted*; the schema first matters at resolution (step\n   167\t   2), where it decides which negative `v`s are tempids and which are plain\n   168\t   negative values. In `:resolved` mode admission *is* `local-datom?`.\n   169\t   Strictness is restored after step 2: every row that reaches step 3\n   170\t   satisfies `local-datom?`. A transaction-record element is admitted by\n   171\t   the same per-datom rule applied to each of its datoms plus the record's\n   172\t   own shape checks (one shared `t`, non-empty); on an `:unresolved` medium\n   173\t   a record's positive-`e` datoms therefore defect the batch through the\n   174\t   mode rule of step 2, which is the intended reading — records are a\n   175\t   transactor's shape, and a transactor resolves. Anything not admitted is a\n   176\t   **defect** — recorded in `:defects` with the batch ordinal and the\n   177\t   element's position — and the whole batch is skipped *without* stopping\n   178\t   the session. (A throwing `load` would leave the cursor on the bad batch\n   179\t   forever — the same head-of-line hazard `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 11\n   180\t   removes. Bad input is data; a bug in the fold itself is the throw.)\n   181\t   `datoms-from-elements` keeps its strict, throwing contract for the\n   182\t   one-shot path; the fold uses an admitting, non-throwing variant of the\n   183\t   same per-element rule.\n   184\t2. **Resolve identity** (§3). `:unresolved` mode: every tempid in the batch\n   185\t   — whether it appears as an `e` or only as a declared-ref `v` (a reference\n   186\t   to an entity the batch never describes) — is mapped through a batch-local\n   187\t   table to a fresh durable id from `:ids`, and the mapping is emitted as\n   188\t   resolution facts. `:resolved` mode: nothing\n   189\t   is allocated; ids pass through. A batch that violates its mode (a tempid\n   190\t   on a `:resolved` medium; a positive `e` on an `:unresolved` one) is a\n   191\t   defect for the whole batch, so a medium can never be half-resolved.\n   192\t3. **Fold.** Rows and resolution facts are `dao.data.btree/conj`ed into the\n   193\t   four trees — a persistent insert that shares every unmodified node with\n   194\t   the previous state. A node created by the insert carries no address mark;\n   195\t   dirtiness *is* that absence, a property of the node and of nothing else\n   196\t   (`btree.cljc` `node-store` skips every child slot that already carries an\n   197\t   address), so §4.1's `store-tree` later emits exactly what this and\n   198\t   subsequent folds created. Covered indexes are sets; a duplicate row is a\n   199\t   no-op (`conj` returns the same set).\n   200\t4. **Advance** `:batch`, and `:ids` in `:unresolved` mode.\n   201\t\n   202\tThe `:indexes` after batch *n* are a pure function of (index state before,\n   203\tbatch), on every host.\n   204\t\n   205\t### 2.3 What the two views mean over each medium\n   206\t\n   207\t`dao.space.query/current` and `history` are unchanged: they are\n   208\tinterpreters over rows, and the index state's trees are a row source.\n   209\t\n   210\t| Medium | `t` in rows | `current` | `history` |\n   211\t|---|---|---|---|\n   212\t| transactor-written (records) | allocated by the writer, monotonic | greatest-`t`-wins per `[e a v]`, retractions removed — full Datomic semantics | exact rows in `t` order |\n   213\t| a medium whose rows carry no writer-allocated `t` (all rows `t 0`, `m default-op`) | not meaningful | the *set* of facts asserted — correct for a medium of assertions with no retractions | degenerate: all rows share `t 0`; order is the observer's batch ordinal, available through resolution facts (§3), **not** through `t` |\n   214\t\n   215\tThe observer does not paper over the second row by minting `t` — decision\n   216\t4. A composition that wants transaction-time semantics over such a medium\n   217\troutes it through a transactor (the writer allocates `t`); one that wants\n   218\t\"what facts does this medium assert\" reads `current` and gets exactly that.\n   219\t**Retractions on such a medium**: an assertion and a later retraction of\n   220\tthe same `[e a v]` both carry `t 0` and differ only in `m`, which\n   221\t`current-state-seq` rejects as conflicting history (`query.cljc`, the\n   222\t`m`-conflict throw). The index does not check for this — deciding whether\n   223\tan `m` value means \"retract\" is payload interpretation, decision 6 — so the\n   224\trows are folded and `history` answers correctly, while `current` over such\n   225\ta medium *throws* at query time. Loud, not silent; a composition that\n   226\tretracts on a medium without writer `t` has chosen the wrong medium for\n   227\t`current`.\n   228\t\n   229\t---\n   230\t\n   231\t## 3. Identity\n   232\t\n   233\t### 3.1 Why the observer must resolve\n   234\t\n   235\tA medium written batch by batch without a transactor carries negative\n   236\ttempids allocated *per batch* (from `(- datom/first-user-id)` downward, by\n   237\tconvention; the observer relies only on their being negative). Two\n   238\tconsecutive batches both contain `-16`. Folding them raw into one index\n   239\twould merge unrelated entities — the silent-wrong-data case.\n   240\t`dao.space.transact` resolves tempids within one transaction; the observer\n   241\tmust do the same per batch, and only the observer can, because only it\n   242\tholds the cross-batch allocator.\n   243\t\n   244\t**Why a mode, fixed at construction.** Durable ids are positive integers on\n   245\tone number line. A medium whose writer allocates positive ids (a transactor's\n   246\tlog) and an observer minting positive ids for tempids cannot share an index\n   247\twithout eventually colliding: the writer emits 100, the observer mints 101,\n   248\tthe writer's next transaction emits 101. No watermark rule fixes this,\n   249\tbecause the two allocators never coordinate. So an index state is either\n   250\t`:resolved` — it indexes media whose ids are already durable and allocates\n   251\tnothing — or `:unresolved` — it indexes media that carry only tempids and\n   252\towns every positive id in its index, allocating from `datom/first-user-id`\n   253\tupward. A composition that needs both kinds of medium in one query opens two\n   254\tindex states as two sources; `dao.space.query` already keeps sources as\n   255\tseparate db-values and joins only where the query says so — and here the\n   256\tquery must *not* equate `?e` across a `:resolved` and an `:unresolved`\n   257\tsource, since both number lines start at `datom/first-user-id` and the same\n   258\tinteger names unrelated entities. Joins across them go through values, never\n   259\tids. Phase 2's shared allocator is therefore a shared allocator across\n   260\t`:unresolved` sessions only.\n   261\t\n   262\t### 3.2 Resolution facts\n   263\t\n   264\tFor every tempid `τ` the observer maps to durable `δ` in batch *n*, it\n   265\tasserts, in its own attribute namespace:\n   266\t\n   267\t```\n   268\t[δ :dao.space.index/batch   n   n default-op]\n   269\t[δ :dao.space.index/tempid  τ   n default-op]\n   270\t```\n   271\t\n   272\tThe row's `t` is the observer's batch ordinal *n* — its own logical clock,\n   273\tnever a host clock, and not the observed rows' `t`. `m` is\n   274\t`datom/default-op`: the attribute namespace alone distinguishes these facts\n   275\tfrom observed rows, and nothing else is needed. That is a **contract on\n   276\tmedia, not a check in the index**: a medium must not assert attributes in\n   277\t`:dao.space.index/*`. The index does not inspect attribute namespaces\n   278\t(decision 6), so a medium that violates the reservation shares `[e a v]`\n   279\tkeys with resolution facts and owns the resulting `current` semantics\n   280\t(greatest-`t` shadowing, or the `m`-conflict throw on a same-`t` meet).\n   281\tWith the reservation honoured, resolution facts and observed rows have\n   282\tdisjoint `[e a v]` keys and can neither shadow nor conflict. (An earlier draft gave the\n   283\tobserver \"its own operation entity\" for `m`; that would have required\n   284\tallocating an entity for the observer itself, and the namespace already\n   285\tdoes the work.) These are facts *about* observation, distinct from the\n   286\tobserved rows. They are what make the following queryable:\n   287\t\n   288\t- **Which batch asserted this entity** — `[?e :dao.space.index/batch ?n]`.\n   289\t- **Cross-medium provenance**, the case `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2 leaves to\n   290\t  \"a composition that commits\". With one index session per medium\n   291\t  (`program-in`, `program-out`, log) sharing an allocator, or one session\n   292\t  over a merged view, the macro expander's event\n   293\t  `[ev :yin/source-batch t] [ev :yin/source-call -16]` joins to the source\n   294\t  entity through `[?e :dao.space.index/batch t'] [?e\n   295\t  :dao.space.index/tempid -16]` — where `t'` is the observer's ordinal\n   296\t  for the source batch the expander numbered `t`. The correspondence\n   297\t  between the expander's `:t` and the observer's ordinal is one more\n   298\t  resolution fact the composition asserts when it wires both observers to\n   299\t  the same medium. It is a constant offset **only while neither session has\n   300\t  seen a `gap`**: a skipped batch on either side shifts every later ordinal\n   301\t  by one, silently. The composition must therefore check both sessions'\n   302\t  `:ingress-gaps` before trusting the offset, and after a gap either\n   303\t  re-derive it or mark provenance across that point as unknown. This is the\n   304\t  same \"an index with a gap is a partial index\" rule as open question 2.\n   305\t\n   306\tRefs are resolved only for attributes the supplied `:schema` declares as\n   307\t`:db.type/ref` — exactly as the transactor relocates only declared refs\n   308\ttoday. The schema is the composition's to supply (for a program medium,\n   309\t`yin.vm/schema`; for an expander's log, `yin.vm.macro/event-schema`);\n   310\tthe index learns which attributes are refs from it and nothing else. An undeclared attribute holding a\n   311\tnegative number is a value, not a ref, and is left alone.\n   312\t\n   313\t### 3.3 What is not promised\n   314\t\n   315\tDurable ids are the *observer's* reading. Two independent observers over the\n   316\tsame medium allocate independently; their ids agree only if they are the\n   317\tsame session or share a checkpoint (§4.2). This is the same statement as\n   318\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2's \"refs across media are meaningful only for durable\n   319\tids\", now with the owner of \"durable\" named: it is whichever dao.space\n   320\tobserver indexed the medium.\n   321\t\n   322\t---\n   323\t\n   324\t## 4. Publication and the checkpoint\n   325\t\n   326\t### 4.1 Incremental publication\n   327\t\n   328\tBecause `:indexes` are persistent `dao.data.btree` values, publishing after\n   329\tbatch *n* is `bt/store-tree` over each tree plus one manifest — the same\n   330\tnode-blob format and intake path `publish-index!` uses today. What makes it\n   331\t*incremental* is that the trees keep their `:storage` across publishes:\n   332\t`store-tree` stores only the **dirty subgraph** (its docstring: \"no-op\n   333\treturning the existing address when the set is already stored\"), so nodes\n   334\tunchanged since the last publication are never re-stored and never\n   335\tre-appended. `publish-index!` today rebuilds into a *fresh* recording\n   336\thandle each time, which is why every node is dirty and every publish is\n   337\tO(tree). Two things persist across publishes in the index state, and they\n   338\tmust not be confused: the **stored-address marks on the trees' nodes**,\n   339\twhich are what `store-tree` consults (dirtiness lives in the tree, not in\n   340\tthe storage), and the **identity of `:storage`**, so that a stored node is\n   341\tnever re-stored under a second handle. What does *not* persist is the\n   342\trecording handle's content: the blobs it recorded since the last flush are\n   343\texactly the payloads to append, in store order, and `flush-staged` drains\n   344\tthem once the intake has accepted the manifest, so the handle never grows\n   345\twith history. Append cost is therefore proportional to what changed;\n   346\t`jing/materialize!`'s dedup at the far end is a second line of defence, not\n   347\tthe mechanism.\n   348\t\n   349\t**Draining is safe only if nothing ever refaults through the drained\n   350\thandle.** `store-tree` needs nothing from it (marks live in the tree), but\n   351\t`-store-tree!` also overwrites the set wrapper's *restore source* with the\n   352\tstorage it was given (`btree.cljc`, `set! storage storage'`), and\n   353\t`resident-root` refaults an evicted root through that field. On the JVM a\n   354\troot restored from a durable store is held by a soft reference (the\n   355\t`kv-storage` default ref-type there); on a resumed checkpoint session the\n   356\tsequence *resume → publish through the recording handle → flush → drain →\n   357\tGC clears the root → next fold or query refaults the root through the\n   358\tdrained handle* ends in \"missing index segment\". Child slots are unaffected\n   359\t(they fault through the durable store the settings name). The invariant the\n   360\tindex state must keep: **a tree's refaults resolve against durable content,\n   361\tnever against the recording handle.** The mechanism is that every tree an\n   362\tindex session holds carries `Settings` with ref-type `:strong`, so a root is\n   363\tnever evicted and never refaults — and *where those settings come from\n   364\tdiffers by session kind*, which is the whole subtlety. Ref-pinning consults\n   365\tthe **tree's** settings, not the storage passed to `store-tree`, so a\n   366\t`:strong` recording handle pins nothing by itself:\n   367\t\n   368\t- a session started at `:oldest` builds its trees either in memory (settings\n   369\t  carry no storage; `make-store-ref` pins the raw node) or through its\n   370\t  recording `kv-storage` constructed with `:ref-type :strong` — safe either\n   371\t  way;\n   372\t- a **resumed** session must restore its trees through a\n   373\t  session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage` over the durable content store\n   374\t  (`bt/restore-tree` directly, behind an index-side helper) — **not**\n   375\t  through `query/open-published!` / `restored-indexes`, whose storage takes\n   376\t  the host default, `:soft` on the JVM, because those are the *read* path\n   377\t  and a query consumer wants eviction. Restoring through the read path would\n   378\t  reproduce the sequence above exactly.\n   379\t\n   380\tRe-restoring the trees from the durable store after each flush was\n   381\tconsidered and rejected — \"append success means enqueued, not materialized\"\n   382\t(`dao.space.index.md`), so the durable store may not yet hold the blobs the\n   383\tnew manifest names, and `-restore` throws on absence. cljs and cljd default\n   384\tto `:strong` already and never see the hazard.\n   385\t\n   386\tThe staging discipline is `yin.vm.macro.md` §5's: node blobs then manifest\n   387\tare staged as one payload list; `full` retains the exact list and leaves the\n   388\tindex state not-ready; `ok` on the manifest clears it. The manifest is always\n   389\tlast, so a partial prefix is retry-safe, as today.\n   390\t\n   391\t### 4.2 The checkpoint the transactor asked for\n   392\t\n   393\t`dao.space.transactor.md` *Open items*: \"the log carries its own checkpoint,\n   394\tfrom which the watermark and incremental indexes resume\". An index state\n   395\tis that checkpoint's shape: `{:manifest-address a :cursor c :ids i\n   396\t:batch n}` — the published trees, the medium position they cover, and the\n   397\tallocator state. A restarted observer restores the four trees lazily from\n   398\tthe manifest through a session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage` over the\n   399\tdurable content store (§4.1 — not the query read path), re-attaches at `c`,\n   400\tand continues folding. Nothing\n   401\tis replayed from the origin. The watermark the transactor derives by\n   402\tscanning the stream becomes `(max t)` over the checkpointed index's rows —\n   403\tstill O(rows), since `t` is not a sort key in any covered order, but it\n   404\ttouches the index rather than replaying the medium, which is the point.\n   405\t\n   406\tTwo caveats bound this. First, a cursor is transport-scoped: it names a\n   407\tposition on *this* handle's logical stream. Over a process-lifetime\n   408\tmemory-log — the transactor's local medium today — a restarted process sees\n   409\t\"a new, empty logical stream with a new identity\" (`dao.space.md`, *Fault\n   410\tTolerance*), so the checkpoint's `:cursor` is meaningless after restart and\n   411\ta resumed observer over such a medium can only start a fresh session at\n   412\t`:oldest` of the new, empty log. Resumption in the sense above needs a\n   413\tmedium whose cursors survive the process (`dao.stream.file.md`), and the\n   414\tcheckpoint is worth recording only for those. Second, whether the checkpoint\n   415\trecord lives on the observed medium itself (the transactor doc's preference:\n   416\t\"one truth\") or beside the manifest in `dao.jing` is left open here; either\n   417\tis a composition choice, and the observer's state is the same value in\n   418\tboth.\n   419\t\n   420\t---\n   421\t\n   422\t## 5. Relationship to the transactor and to `publish-index!`\n   423\t\n   424\tThe transactor remains the writer and the owner of `t`. \"Indexing is the\n   425\twriter's duty\" becomes: *the writer runs `dao.space.index` as an observer\n   426\tover its own stream* — one index state attached to its local memory-log,\n   427\tpublishing on its policy. `publish-index!` is then a convenience over the\n   428\tsame functions: attach at `:oldest`, `fold-batch` to `blocked`, publish\n   429\tonce, discard the state. Its full-history behaviour is preserved as the\n   430\tdegenerate case of an observer that never kept state, and its manifest\n   431\tformat, intake path, and retry-safety are unchanged.\n   432\t\n   433\t`dao.space.index` gains `fold-batch`, `publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`,\n   434\tthe admitting per-element rule, and the resolution-fact attributes; it loses\n   435\tnothing. Nothing about `dao.jing`\n   436\tchanges: it still materializes opaque payloads from intake pools and never\n   437\tinterprets. Nothing about `dao.space.query` changes: the index state's trees\n   438\tare one more row source, alongside relation values and published\n   439\tmanifests. The three boundaries of `dao.space.md` are untouched;\n   440\twhat moves is *when* the transactor-side index is built (continuously,\n   441\tnot at publish) and *over what* (any medium, not only the writer's own).\n   442\t\n   443\t---\n   444\t\n   445\t## 6. Invariants\n   446\t\n   447\t| Invariant | How the observer honours it |\n   448\t|---|---|\n   449\t| No hidden global state | Allocator, batch ordinal, trees, staged publication — all fields of one index-state value threaded through `run-on-stream`. No registry of observers; a composition holds the states it created. |\n   450\t| No implicit control flow | Indexing happens in exactly one place: `fold-batch` inside the observe step. Nothing indexes on write, on query, or on publish. |\n   451\t| No callbacks | `fold-batch` is a function (index-state, batch) → index-state'. The driver is `run-on-stream`; cadence is the composition's. |\n   452\t| No shared mutable state | `dao.data.btree` values are persistent; two sessions never share a tree by reference they could both mutate. The recording content handle at publish time is created per publication. |\n   453\t| No layer collapsing | *append* (writer) → medium → *observe/fold* (this note) → *publish* (intake) → *materialize* (`dao.jing`) → *query*. The VM is a sibling observer, not a stage. |\n   454\t| No assumed graphs | Ref resolution uses only the supplied `:schema`; the observer does not infer which values are refs. Batch shape is validated by `datoms-from-elements`; malformed elements are defects, not guesses. |\n   455\t\n   456\t---\n   457\t\n   458\t## 7. Phases\n   459\t\n   460\t**Phase 0 — relocate the coordination loop (done 2026-09-13).** The\n   461\t`attach`/`observe-next`/`run-on-stream` loop lived at\n   462\t`yin.vm.stream-observer` because the VM was its first consumer, but its\n   463\town docstring says it inspects no evaluator field, and a `dao.space`\n   464\tnamespace requiring `yin.vm.*` would invert the layering (`dao.stream` →\n   465\t`dao.space` → `yin.vm`, never back). It is now `dao.stream.observer`\n   466\t(`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc`, test\n   467\t`test/dao/stream/observer_test.cljc`), beside `dao.stream.observe`\n   468\twhose single `step` it loops over. The `yin.vm` evaluators, the REPL, and\n   469\tthe macro expander require it from there; `yin.vm` keeps no observer code\n   470\tof its own. The pending fix from `yin.vm.macro.md` §5 — keep the throw,\n   471\tcarry the partial `{:observer :consumer}` session in `ex-data` — lands in this\n   472\tnamespace.\n   473\t\n   474\t**Phase 0′ — parity over the transactor's medium.** `dao.space.index` gains\n   475\t`fold-batch`, `publish!`, `flush-staged`, `drain`, the admitting element\n   476\trule, and the resolution facts; a `:resolved` session attached at `:oldest`\n   477\tover a transactor's local memory-log produces `:indexes` equal (as sets) to\n   478\t`index-datoms` over `snapshot-datoms` of the same log, and a one-shot\n   479\t`publish!` equal (as a manifest) to `publish-index!`. Tests: set equality on\n   480\tall four trees; manifest equality; a malformed element becomes a defect and\n   481\tthe next batch still folds; a tempid on a `:resolved` medium is a defect;\n   482\t`run-on-stream` `blocked`/`end` behaviour; a second `publish!` after more\n   483\tbatches appends only the blobs stored since the first (count them); the\n   484\t**checkpoint value** `{:manifest-address :cursor :ids :batch}` round-trips\n   485\tthrough `pr-str`/`read-string` (it, unlike a live session, is plain data —\n   486\ta `BTSet` prints elements-only and a storage handle does not print at all),\n   487\tand `restored-indexes` over the published manifest equals the live trees as\n   488\tsets; the F2 hazard pinned deterministically both ways through the btree's\n   489\texisting `:test` ref seam (`:test` ref-type plus `clear-test-refs!`, which\n   490\texists because soft clearing cannot be forced): a resumed session restored\n   491\tthrough a `:test`-ref storage, published, drained, refs cleared → the next\n   492\tquery *throws* \"missing index segment\" (the hazard reproduced); the same\n   493\tsequence with the session-constructed `:strong` storage → no throw; and a\n   494\tsession restored through `restored-indexes` is asserted to carry\n   495\t`bt/default-ref-type*` rather than `:strong` — an assertion that is\n   496\tmeaningful only where the host default is not already `:strong`, so it is\n   497\tgated to the JVM (on cljs and cljd the read path *is* `:strong` and the\n   498\thazard does not exist).\n   499\t\n   500\t**Phase 1 — a medium with batch-local tempids (composition test).** The\n   501\tindex is exercised over a medium another observer also reads: two\n   502\t`dao.stream.observer` sessions on one `program-out`, one driving\n   503\t`ast-walker`, one driving `dao.space.index` in `:unresolved` mode. The\n   504\tindex code under test knows nothing of the VM; the test does. Tests:\n   505\tnegative `e` rows are admitted and resolved, positive `e` on this medium is\n   506\ta defect; the VM's value and the\n   507\tindex's `current` view from the same batches; after each appended batch,\n   508\twith no snapshot call, `q` over the index state already sees that batch's\n   509\tentities; two batches with overlapping\n   510\ttempids index as distinct entities with correct resolution facts;\n   511\t`[?e :yin/type :lambda]` finds every lambda the program contained; a ref\n   512\tattribute resolved, an undeclared negative value untouched; `history`\n   513\tdocumented as degenerate over `t 0` rows.\n   514\t\n   515\t**Phase 2 — cross-medium provenance.** Observers over `program-in`,\n   516\t`program-out`, and the macro log with a shared allocator; the offset fact\n   517\tbetween the expander's `:t` and the observer's ordinal. Test: the\n   518\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 query chain source-call → event → expansion-root\n   519\tanswered by `dao.space.query/q` over the three indexed media.\n   520\t\n   521\t**Phase 3 — checkpoint over a durable medium.** Over a file-backed medium\n   522\t(`dao.stream.file`), restart from `{:manifest-address :cursor :ids :batch}`\n   523\tand continue; the resumed index equals a from-origin index. Over a\n   524\tmemory-log, assert the documented behaviour instead: a resumed session\n   525\tstarts fresh at `:oldest` of the new log and the checkpoint is not\n   526\tconsulted. The transactor's `create!` derives its watermark from a\n   527\tcheckpoint when one is offered.\n   528\t\n   529\t---\n   530\t\n   531\t## 8. Open questions\n   532\t\n   533\t- **One session per medium, or one over a merged view?** Per medium keeps\n   534\t  each cursor honest and makes `gap` attributable; a merged view needs a\n   535\t  merge interpreter that `dao.stream.md` does not (and should not) provide.\n   536\t  This note leans per-medium with a shared allocator, but the allocator\n   537\t  then couples the sessions — a composition choice to state when it is\n   538\t  made.\n   539\t- **Retention and `gap`.** Over an evicting transport the observer inherits\n   540\t  `dao.stream.observer`'s gap accounting; an index with a gap is a partial\n   541\t  index and must say so, and a cross-session ordinal offset (§3.2) is void\n   542\t  after one. Whether a published manifest should carry the gap count is\n   543\t  undecided.\n   544\t- **Where the checkpoint lives** (§4.2).\n   545\t- **Whether `snapshot` is retired** in favour of \"run a throwaway session to\n   546\t  `blocked` and take its trees\". Probably, but `snapshot`'s status\n   547\t  vocabulary (`:ended`/`:blocked`/`:gap`/`:defect`) should survive as the\n   548\t  session's terminal report.\n   549\t\n   550\t---\n   551\t\n   552\t## Appendix A. Review findings and their resolution\n   553\t\n   554\tRound 1 (`collab/1789289033041-runtime-review-index-as-observer.glm-5.3.findings.md`), on `5296ee5`; glm-5.3 verified every mechanism claim against the code (all nine hold, claim 9 partially) and requested changes on:\n   555\t\n   556\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   557\t|---|---|---|\n   558\t| F1 | Phase 0′'s `pr-str`/`read-string` round trip of a live session cannot pass: `BTSet` prints elements-only, a storage handle is unprintable (glm P2) | Phase 0′ round-trips the *checkpoint value* instead and compares `restored-indexes` to the live trees |\n   559\t| F2 | Draining the recording handle is unsafe on the resumed-checkpoint path on the JVM: `-store-tree!` rewrites the wrapper's restore source and a soft-ref'd root refaults through the drained handle (glm P2) | §4.1 states the invariant (refaults resolve against durable content, never the handle) and pins index-session roots `:strong`; re-restore after flush rejected because materialization is asynchronous; Phase 0′ test |\n   560\t| F3 | The `:dao.space.index/*` reservation is a convention the index cannot check (glm P3) | §3.2 states it as a contract on media, with the consequence of violating it |\n   561\t| F4 | Assert-then-retract on a `t 0` medium hits `current`'s `m`-conflict throw (glm P3) | §2.3 states it: folded, `history` correct, `current` throws loudly; not a defect, since recognising a retraction is payload interpretation |\n   562\t| F5 | \"A query, not a scan\" overstates; \"dirty-tracked against storage\" misplaces dirtiness; tx records and `v`-only tempids on `:unresolved` media inferred, not stated (glm P3) | §4.2 and §2.2 reworded; both cases stated in §2.2 steps 1–2 |\n   563\t\n   564\tRound 2 (`collab/1789289033041-runtime-review-index-as-observer-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md`), on `280dda2`; F1, F3, F4, F5 confirmed resolved, F2 partially:\n   565\t\n   566\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   567\t|---|---|---|\n   568\t| N1 | The `:strong` remedy binds to the session's recording storage, but ref-pinning consults the *tree's* settings; a session resumed via `open-published!`/`restored-indexes` gets the JVM `:soft` default and the F2 sequence survives (glm P2) | §4.1 states where settings come from per session kind; a resumed session restores through a session-constructed `:strong` `kv-storage`, never the query read path; §4.2 reworded |\n   569\t| N2 | \"Survives a forced GC\" is not a deterministic test; soft clearing cannot be forced (glm P3) | Phase 0′ pins the hazard both ways through the `:test` ref-type and `clear-test-refs!` |\n   570\t\n   571\tRound 3 (`collab/1789289033041-runtime-review-index-as-observer-r3.glm-5.3.findings.md`), on `0bd7550` — **APPROVE** (glm-5.3); N1, N2 confirmed resolved and the resumed path verified closed against `btree.cljc` with no other refault route:\n   572\t\n   573\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   574\t|---|---|---|\n   575\t| N3 | The \"wrong construction\" assertion is vacuous off the JVM, where the read path already defaults `:strong` (glm P3) | Phase 0′ asserts against `bt/default-ref-type*` and gates that assertion to the JVM |\n   576\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
